kind: fix
upstream: not yet reported to xatlas (whose last code change was in 2022).

Lost wakeup: the task scheduler sets a worker's `wakeup` flag and notifies its
condition variable without holding the worker's mutex, so a worker that has
just checked the flag and is about to block can miss the notify and sleep
forever, hanging `join()` or a task. Workers hold that mutex while they run
tasks, so taking it to set the flag would deadlock nested tasks. Instead a
worker re-checks the flag every millisecond, bounding any lost wakeup to 1 ms;
the cost is an idle worker waking 1,000 times a second.

Evidence: found by fuzz/xatlas.cpp as rare multi-second hangs (2026-10). It is
a timing race, so it can't be reproduced on demand: the mechanism is the
unlocked flag-and-notify in the code below.
Covered by: fuzz/xatlas.cpp, whose -timeout catches a hang.

--- a/source/xatlas/xatlas.cpp
+++ b/source/xatlas/xatlas.cpp
@@ -3270,7 +3270,9 @@
 		m_threadIndex = threadIndex;
 		std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(worker->mutex);
 		for (;;) {
-			worker->cv.wait(lock, [=]{ return worker->wakeup.load(); });
+			// trimkit (vendor/patches/xatlas-lost-wakeup.patch): the flag is set and notified without
+			// this mutex, so a wakeup can be lost; re-checking every millisecond bounds the wait
+			worker->cv.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::milliseconds(1), [=]{ return worker->wakeup.load(); });
 			worker->wakeup = false;
